        "Considering a historic home in Canemah, Oregon City? The character is real: a median build year of 1937, some homes dating to the 1800s, set above the Willamette River near Willamette Falls, with Canemah Bluff Nature Park's trails and river overlooks close by. What it involves: preservation guidelines may require additional review for exterior changes as part of the historic district designation, and older systems (wiring, plumbing, mechanical) may need updating depending on the property's history. Touring with an inspector experienced in historic Oregon City homes tends to surface the real condition questions faster than a listing description can. The full breakdown and current listings are on the site.",
      variant: "considerations",
      exp: { title: "Canemah in Oregon City: Living With History Above the Falls", teaser: "Canemah's past is the main attraction, and a true historic district comes with duties you should understand first. I cover preservation rules, upkeep and the commute. The post also looks at homes with a median build year of 1937, a few from the 1800s, and the bluff park with trails and river overlooks." },
      faq: [
        { q: "Is Canemah a historic district?", a: "Yes. The Canemah Historic District was added to the National Register of Historic Places on October 11, 1978. According to the City of Oregon City, the district covers about 63.71 acres on the south bank of the Willamette River just above the falls, with the river as its north edge and the bluffs to the south. It includes 41 blocks, 37 of them from the original 1850 plat and 4 from the 1891 First Addition. The city describes Canemah as one of only a few intact former riverboat towns remaining in Oregon.", sources: ["https://orcity.org/1236", "https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/78002279"] },
        { q: "Can you remodel a house in the Canemah historic district?", a: "Yes, but exterior changes usually need city review first. Oregon City says interior alterations do not require Historic Review Board review, and ordinary maintenance or repairs using identical materials and designs need no application. Changes to windows, doors, siding or other exterior features go through staff review when they meet the board's adopted policies, or a Type III Historic Review Board hearing when they don't. Additions over 30 percent of the original structure, all new construction and all demolition in Canemah require board review. The Planning Division (503-722-3789) can confirm what a specific project needs.", sources: ["https://orcity.org/1295", "https://orcity.org/802/Historic-Preservation", "https://orcity.org/1296/Historic-Review-Board-Policies"] },
        { q: "Is Canemah in a flood zone?", a: "It depends on the exact property. Canemah's original riverboat landing sat close to river level and was largely wiped out by the December 1861 flood, while the district today rises from the riverbank toward the bluffs to the south. Oregon City participates in the National Flood Insurance Program and publishes a map showing the FEMA 100-year floodplain along with the extent of the 1996 flood. To check a specific address, look it up on FEMA's Flood Map Service Center and the city's floodplain map, and ask the city's Planning Division about any floodplain overlay rules that apply.", sources: ["https://orcity.org/422/FEMA-Floodplain", "https://orcity.org/678/Water-Quality-Flood-Management-Areas", "https://msc.fema.gov/portal/search"] },
        { q: "When was Canemah founded and when did it become part of Oregon City?", a: "Canemah was established in 1845 at a landing where cargo and passengers were portaged around Willamette Falls. The name is often translated as \"canoe place.\" Absalom Hedges laid out the townsite, and the steamboat Canemah was launched there in 1851, the start of a riverboat building era that lasted into the 1870s. The opening of the Willamette Falls Locks in 1873 let steamers bypass the portage, and Canemah declined as a river port. Oregon City annexed Canemah in 1928.", sources: ["https://orcity.org/1236", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canemah,_Oregon"] },
        { q: "What school district serves Canemah in Oregon City?", a: "Canemah is within Oregon City School District 62. The district runs several K-5 elementary schools, two middle schools (Gardiner Middle School and Tumwata Middle School) and Oregon City High School. Which elementary and middle school a home is assigned to depends on its exact address, so check the district's boundary maps or call the district transportation department, which handles questions for addresses near a boundary line, before relying on any listing description.", sources: ["https://ocsd62.org/boundary-maps", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oregon_City_School_District_(Oregon)"] },
        { q: "Can you bring a dog to Canemah Bluff Nature Park?", a: "No. Metro does not allow pets in Canemah Bluff Nature Park because they can damage sensitive habitat. Leashed pets are allowed at the adjacent city park run by Oregon City, which has restrooms, a playground, a basketball court and a reservable picnic shelter. The nature park is open sunrise to sunset, with free parking at 917 4th Ave. and more than a mile of unpaved trails through Oregon white oak prairie and forest to a Willamette River viewpoint. The Canemah Historic Pioneer Cemetery next to the park is private and kept locked.", sources: ["https://www.oregonmetro.gov/parks/canemah-bluff-nature-park"] },
        { q: "Are the Willamette Falls Locks near Canemah open?", a: "Not as of the most recent reporting. The locks, which opened in 1873, have been closed since 2011, when the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers found corroded gates and seismic concerns. Repair work is moving in phases under the Willamette Falls Locks Authority, starting with seismic and gate stability improvements, but OPB reported in July 2025 that the Legislature ended its session without the funding needed to finish the job, and no firm reopening date has been announced. Check the Locks Authority or local news for the current status.", sources: ["https://www.opb.org/article/2025/07/21/willamette-falls-locks-navigational-boats-infrastructure-seismic-earthquake/", "https://westlinnoregon.gov/planning/willamette-falls-locks", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canemah,_Oregon"] },
      ],
