      faq: [
        { q: "Is Rock Creek in Happy Valley city limits?", a: "Mostly. The City of Happy Valley's Rock Creek Comprehensive Plan, adopted June 5, 2001 and amended several times since, covers about 1,012 acres, and the plan states that the majority of that area is within Happy Valley city limits. The planning area sits next to Sunnyside Village and the southeast edge of the Portland metro urban growth boundary, and it includes the part of Sunnyside Village inside the city. Because the plan places only a majority of the area inside the city, confirm the jurisdiction for any specific address with the city or county records.", sources: ["https://www.happyvalleyor.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/RockCreekCompPlan-electronic-file-Amended-10-4-16-1.pdf"] },
        { q: "What school district serves Rock Creek in Happy Valley?", a: "Rock Creek is in North Clackamas School District 12. The district lists two feeder systems serving much of Happy Valley: Clackamas High School, fed by Rock Creek Middle School and Mt. Scott, Oregon Trail, Spring Mountain and Sunnyside elementary schools, and Adrienne C. Nelson High School, fed by Happy Valley Middle School and Beatrice Morrow Cannady, Happy Valley, Verne Duncan and Scouters Mountain elementary schools. Note that Rock Creek Middle School's name does not decide assignment. Enter the exact address in the district's School Locator to see which schools apply.", sources: ["https://happyvalleyms.nclack.k12.or.us/about-us/school-boundaries", "https://www.happyvalleyor.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/RockCreekCompPlan-electronic-file-Amended-10-4-16-1.pdf"] },
        { q: "Does TriMet run buses through Rock Creek in Happy Valley?", a: "Yes. TriMet Line 155 Sunnyside runs along SE Sunnyside Road between Clackamas Town Center Transit Center and SE 172nd Avenue, with weekday, Saturday and Sunday schedules. Line 156 Mather Rd offers weekday only service between Clackamas Town Center and Happy Valley via Mather Road and Highway 212/224. Since the August 23, 2026 service change, the MAX Green Line at Clackamas Town Center runs only to Gateway Transit Center, so trips into downtown Portland now involve a transfer. Check TriMet's trip planner for current stops and times.", sources: ["https://trimet.org/schedules/r155.htm", "https://trimet.org/servicechanges/2026august.htm"] },
        { q: "How long is the commute from Rock Creek to downtown Portland?", a: "It varies widely with time of day and route. By car, the usual approach is west on Sunnyside Road or Highway 212 to I-205, then north toward I-84 or through Milwaukie on Highway 224. Drive times change a lot with rush hour traffic, so test the route at the hour you would travel. By transit, TriMet scheduled the extended Line 40 trip from Clackamas Town Center to downtown at 49 minutes, on weekdays only, plus the bus ride from Rock Creek to the transit center.", sources: ["https://news.trimet.org/2026/08/signs-thousands-of-them-remind-riders-that-trimet-service-is-changing-on-aug-23/", "https://trimet.org/servicechanges/2026august.htm"] },
        { q: "Can you bring a dog to Mount Talbert or Scouters Mountain nature parks?", a: "No. Metro bans pets at both parks to protect sensitive habitat and wildlife. Mount Talbert Nature Park centers on a 750 foot volcanic butte with more than four miles of trails, a main entrance at 10945 SE Mather Road and a second entrance south of SE Sunnyside Road at 117th Avenue. Scouters Mountain Nature Park, at 11000 SE Honorable Way, has a quarter mile loop and a steeper mile long loop, Mount Hood views and a reservable picnic shelter. Both are open sunrise to sunset with free parking.", sources: ["https://www.oregonmetro.gov/parks/mount-talbert-nature-park", "https://www.oregonmetro.gov/places/scouters-mountain-nature-park"] },
        { q: "Where does the name Rock Creek come from in Happy Valley?", a: "The area takes its name from Rock Creek, the stream that runs through it. Happy Valley's Rock Creek Comprehensive Plan calls the densely forested Rock Creek corridor the planning area's most significant natural resource and its main north to south route for local wildlife. Four tributaries flow into the creek from the west. The terrain runs from nearly flat south of Sunnyside Road to slopes over 30 percent north of the Sunnyside Village commercial area, and the plan notes views of Mount Hood and the Cascades from higher ground.", sources: ["https://www.happyvalleyor.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/RockCreekCompPlan-electronic-file-Amended-10-4-16-1.pdf"] },
        { q: "Who provides water and sewer service in Rock Creek, Happy Valley?", a: "For addresses inside Happy Valley, the city lists Sunrise Water Authority as a water provider through an intergovernmental agreement, and Water Environment Services, a department of Clackamas County, provides sanitary sewer and storm sewer service under a separate agreement with the city. Because water districts and city boundaries don't always line up, confirm the actual providers for a specific address with the seller's utility bills or the providers directly.", sources: ["https://www.happyvalleyor.gov/?p=1356"] },
      ],
      intro: [
        "Rock Creek's practical reputation is well-earned, but it's worth understanding how it compares to Happy Valley's newer developments before you tour.",
      ],
