        "If you're moving to the Happy Valley, OR area, Scouters Mountain is worth researching: a new-home community, including homes by Holt Homes, built right up to the edge of Scouters Mountain Nature Park, a real, 100-acre, Metro-managed park on a former Boy Scouts campsite with forest trails, Mt. Hood views and a reservable picnic shelter. Nearby recreation also includes Eagle Landing Golf and Hood View Sports Park, and the community is in the North Clackamas School District. If you're comparing Scouters Mountain homes for sale or Happy Valley OR homes for sale more broadly, the full neighborhood guide covers the park access, schools and current listings.",
      variant: "guide",
      exp: { title: "Scouters Mountain in Happy Valley: New Homes Beside a Nature Park", teaser: "These new-construction homes, including Holt Homes builds, sit right against Scouters Mountain Nature Park, a 100-acre park run by Metro on an old Boy Scouts campsite. The post covers forest trails, Mt. Hood views, the reservable picnic shelter, park rules, and two other recreation spots close by, Hood View Sports Park and Eagle Landing Golf." },
      faq: [
        { q: "What school district is Scouters Mountain in, and how do I confirm the assigned schools?", a: "The area is served by North Clackamas School District. The district's boundary page lists Scouters Mountain Elementary, Happy Valley Middle School and Adrienne C. Nelson High School in the same feeder pattern. Assignment depends on the exact address, so enter it in the district's School Locator, a Metro mapping tool linked from the boundary page. The district notes the locator can show an incorrect or conflicting result near boundary lines, so call (503) 353-6000 to confirm before relying on it.", sources: ["https://www.nclack.k12.or.us/enrollment/school-boundaries"] },
        { q: "What are the hours and rules at Scouters Mountain Nature Park, and can I reserve the picnic shelter?", a: "Metro's field guide says the park is open sunrise to sunset, with no dogs or alcohol allowed. Amenities listed are hiking trails, parking, restrooms and a reservable picnic shelter. To reserve the shelter, call 503-665-4995 and choose option 0, or email metropicnicreservations@oregonmetro.gov. The park sits at the top of Boy Scouts Lodge Road in Happy Valley, just off SE 145th Avenue. Rules can change, so check Metro's park page before a visit.", sources: ["https://www.hikespeak.com/pdf/Scouters-Mountain-Field-Guide.pdf"] },
        { q: "How did Scouters Mountain become a nature park, and what is it made of?", a: "According to Metro's field guide, Metro acquired the site in 2011 and spent three years restoring about 100 acres of Douglas fir, bigleaf maple and Oregon white oak forest. Roughly half the forest holds trees more than a century old, and Metro planted about 30,000 native trees and shrubs. The mountain is an extinct lava dome, part of the Boring Lava Field. Beams in the picnic shelter were salvaged from the Chief Obie Lodge, a Boy Scout camp landmark that closed in 2004.", sources: ["https://www.hikespeak.com/pdf/Scouters-Mountain-Field-Guide.pdf"] },
        { q: "Is Scouters Mountain inside Happy Valley city limits, and who manages the land around it?", a: "A 2014 regional trail plan places the stretch that includes the Metro-owned Scouters Mountain summit within the City of Happy Valley. The plan lists Metro, the City of Happy Valley, North Clackamas Parks and Recreation District and Clackamas County among the partner agencies in the area. Parts of the surrounding landscape are unincorporated Clackamas County, so jurisdiction can differ by parcel. Confirm which city or county handles permits and services for a specific address with the City of Happy Valley or Clackamas County.", sources: ["https://www.happyvalleyor.gov/wp-content/uploads/2016/09/MSSMTLMP-Final-Text.pdf"] },
        { q: "Are there building limits on steep slopes or natural resource areas near Scouters Mountain?", a: "The 2014 Mount Scott and Scouters Mountain trail plan describes several Happy Valley overlays. The Steep Slopes Development Overlay limits development on slopes to reduce seismic and landslide hazards, and areas with slopes over 25 percent may not be developed. A Natural Resource Overlay Zone and Metro's Title 13 habitat rules also apply in sensitive areas. Whether any of these touch a given lot depends on the address and on current city code, so ask Happy Valley planning staff before assuming.", sources: ["https://www.happyvalleyor.gov/wp-content/uploads/2016/09/MSSMTLMP-Final-Text.pdf"] },
        { q: "Is there a regional trail planned to link Scouters Mountain with other parks?", a: "Yes, as a proposal. The 2014 Mount Scott and Scouters Mountain Trail Loop Master Plan describes a roughly 37.5 mile loop connecting the Springwater Corridor to the Clackamas River and linking Powell Butte, Buttes Natural Area, Mount Talbert and Scouters Mountain Nature Park. The plan said implementation could take 20 to 25 years and noted no dedicated funding at that time. Alignments were conceptual, so ask Metro or the City of Happy Valley what has been built or funded since.", sources: ["https://www.happyvalleyor.gov/wp-content/uploads/2016/09/MSSMTLMP-Final-Text.pdf"] },
        { q: "What other parks are close to Scouters Mountain?", a: "The 2014 trail plan describes two nearby parks. Happy Valley Park, a 32 acre park on Ridgecrest Road, listed sport courts and fields, a walking loop, a splash pad, an off-leash dog area, a skatepark, a playground and 24 acres of wetlands with boardwalks. Hood View Park, a 35 acre North Clackamas Parks and Recreation District park off 162nd Avenue, listed four all-weather ballfields with views of Mt. Hood and Mt. St. Helens. Amenities may have changed since the plan, so check current park pages.", sources: ["https://www.happyvalleyor.gov/wp-content/uploads/2016/09/MSSMTLMP-Final-Text.pdf"] },
      ],
